## 2. Per-issue classification

Each issue has one whole-issue class. A repaired subclaim is disclosed without discarding its surviving remainder. STALE means the contradicting current condition was read this pass; it does not mean a runtime gate was run. DUPLICATE means the quoted overlap, not that either defect is healthy. FIXED-NEEDS-VERIFICATION records a landing claim without the complete matching acceptance receipt located here. UNCLASSIFIABLE specifies the missing discriminator; old age or a short clean sample is not a fix. The six class names below are exactly the enumerated classes in the brief (which called them 'seven'). All commands are future verification instructions, not executed results.

File citations are repository-relative; kernel shorthand has been expanded where a unique source was identifiable. For timing-sensitive investigations use GDB/register/stack inspection or existing bounded tracing; do not add diagnostic I/O to interrupt/syscall hot paths. Tier-1 handler.rs is not handlers.rs. Any later Tier-1 edit needs explicit approval; Tier-2 edits need the required justification/approval under the applicable repository rule. No such edit is authorized by this plan.

### #420 — NB-12: bssh publickey client authentication

**FIXED-NEEDS-VERIFICATION.** Cells: `24/aarch64`.

`166a2f0b` implements publickey authentication; `libs/libbreenix/src/ssh/auth.rs:404` signs session-bound data and :422 sends it, while `userspace/programs/src/bssh.rs:88–108` selects publickey/wrong-key paths. No retained right-key/wrong-key end-to-end pair was found for the body's explicit request.

Disposition / limit: Verify against a provisioned SSH endpoint/key; endpoint and keys are required inputs, not invented values.

Verification / oracle: Inside guest, `/bin/bssh HOST PORT USER --publickey --smoke` must print `bssh: smoke success` and exit 0; same endpoint with `--publickey-wrong --smoke` must print authentication failure and exit nonzero. Replace HOST/PORT/USER with recorded provisioned fixture, not a guessed host.

