### #493 — check_signals_for_eintr ignores signal disposition — default-ignored signals spuriously interrupt kernel waits

**LIVE-DEFECT.** Cells: `08/x86`, `08/aarch64`, `08/blended`, `10/x86`, `10/aarch64`, `10/blended`, `17/aarch64`.

The original EINTR claim is contradicted by `kernel/src/signal/types.rs:203–210` (`has_interrupting_signals`, default-Ignore excluded) and `kernel/src/syscall/mod.rs:585` calls it. However #493's body also explicitly retains “GPU_PCI_LOCK held across the completion wait” and “Surviving timeout-path free-while-device-owned.” `kernel/src/drivers/virtio/gpu_pci.rs:2183–2188` holds `GPU_PCI_LOCK` across `f(state)`; :2428 waits uninterruptibly and :2433 returns a timeout error. Whole issue is not stale; its signal-only attribution needs an explicit scope disposition.

Smallest repair / scope: Separate GPU submission ownership from waiter lifetime; release the device lock before sleeping and reclaim DMA buffers only after device completion or proven reset. This is a redesign, not a one-line predicate patch. Signal-only cells still carry the open multi-claim issue under the literal rule.

Verification / oracle: Run `bash scripts/run-structure-tests.sh signal_eintr_predicate_structure` for the repaired predicate. GPU residual requires a new deterministic stalled-ctrlq/timeout ownership test: DMA remains owned through timeout, another caller can progress, and completion/reset reclaims once. `bash scripts/parallels/launcher-smoke.sh` is only a smoke supplement.

Known implementation estimate: 16 h.

